(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608197000")
  (:adr/title "the generator said 0 pages differ; it had compared none of them")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19")
  (:adr/tags "generators evidence-floor false-clean appview co-scientist")
  (:adr/body
  "## What happened

With `verify-appview-page-summary` answering again (ADR-2608196900) and reporting
296 findings, the next step was the repair path the detector advertises:

    nbb scripts/gen-appview-page-summary.cljs --names <repo>          ; dry run
    nbb scripts/gen-appview-page-summary.cljs --names <repo> --apply

Run exactly as documented, it said:

    gen-appview-page-summary: 128 page(s) carry a summary; 0 differ from their wrangler

Two tools comparing the same two things, disagreeing completely: **296 findings
against 0 differences.** Checked by hand, the detector was right --
`app-saiban`'s page carries `\"routeCount\": 0, \"routes\": [], \"vars\": []` while its
`wrangler.jsonc` declares `sb4n0j1c.etzhayyim.com/*` and ten vars.

## Why

`repo-of` returns `orgs/<org>/<name>`, and the filter was
`(contains? only repo)`. `--names app-saiban` therefore matched **nothing**, and a
filter that matches nothing produced a report that reads exactly like a clean
fleet. Run with the long spelling, the same command finds the difference:

    --names app-saiban                      0 differ    (compared 0)
    --names orgs/cloud-itonami/app-saiban   1 differ    (compared 1)
    (no --names)                          128 differ    (compared 128)

The `128 page(s) carry a summary` on the same line is the **unfiltered** count, so
the sentence read \"128 examined, all agree\" while nothing had been opened.

## Change

1. `--names` accepts both spellings -- the documented `app-saiban` and the path
   `orgs/cloud-itonami/app-saiban`.
2. The header prints **`N compared`** between the candidate count and the
   difference count. Zero comparisons can no longer hide behind a large candidate
   count.
3. `--names` matching **nothing** exits 2 with `CANNOT ANSWER`, naming what it
   looked for.
4. Names that matched nothing **while others matched** print a ⚠ line. Not a
   refusal -- something was compared -- but a typo in a list is how a repository
   somebody believes they repaired never gets opened.

## Both directions

    --names app-saiban              1 compared, 1 differ, exit 0   (was 0 differ)
    --names app-saiban,no-such-repo 1 compared, 1 differ, exit 0
                                    ⚠ --names entries that matched no page: no-such-repo
    --names nope                    CANNOT ANSWER, exit 2          (was 0 differ, exit 0)
    (no --names)                  128 compared, 128 differ         unchanged

## Why this one matters more than a flag bug

This is the **repair** side, not the measuring side. Every previous iteration this
week found a check that could not fail; this is a fix that could not fix, and
reports success in the same words as a real one. Someone repairing a page,
following the documented usage, would have been told there was nothing to repair --
by the very tool written to repair it.

## Not done here

The 128 pages are still unrepaired. `--apply` writes into the tree it is pointed
at, and pointing it at the shared checkout is exactly what this workspace forbids;
each repair also needs a child-repo commit, a server-side merge and a west pin
advance. That is ~5 operations across ~113 repositories and belongs in its own
scoped piece of work, not appended to a flag fix.")))
